      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_CONCEPTS_01",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__5F4143EE_1",
          "title": "1. Key Academic Principles: Acceleration Due to Gravity and Its Variation with Altitude and Depth",
          "contentMarkdown": "The acceleration due to gravity at Earth's surface of mass M_e and radius R_e is g = G * M_e / R_e^2. At height h above Earth's surface, acceleration due to gravity decreases according to g(h) = g * [1 - 2*h/R_e]. At depth d below Earth's surface, acceleration due to gravity decreases linearly according to g(d) = g * [1 - d/R_e]. The acceleration due to gravity is maximum at Earth's surface and becomes exactly zero at the centre of the Earth.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_EFC26508",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_0E987CEF",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_5ED99053",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_5A4C9B7F"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_CONCEPTS_02",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__5F4143EE_2",
          "title": "2. Key Academic Principles: Acceleration Due to Gravity and Its Variation with Altitude and Depth",
          "contentMarkdown": "Due to Earth's rotation, effective acceleration due to gravity at latitude lambda is g_eff = g - omega^2 * R_e * cos^2(lambda). Acceleration due to gravity on Earth is maximum at the poles and minimum at the equator. Variation with Altitude (Height h above surface): For h << R_e: g(h) ~= g * [1 - 2*h/R_e].",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_A4B953D2",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_B0349097",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_BFF1F70D",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_044F54FF"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_CONCEPTS_03",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__5F4143EE_3",
          "title": "3. Key Academic Principles: Acceleration Due to Gravity and Its Variation with Altitude and Depth",
          "contentMarkdown": "Thus, g decreases linearly with height for small altitudes. Variation with Latitude (due to Earth's rotation with angular speed omega): At equator (lambda = 0 deg): g_equator = g - omega^2 * R_e (minimum). At poles (lambda = 90 deg): g_pole = g (maximum).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_B06AA787",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_6C0BEF03",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_0DF94E44",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_2829012F"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_CONCEPTS_04",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__5F4143EE_4",
          "title": "4. Key Academic Principles: Acceleration Due to Gravity and Its Variation with Altitude and Depth",
          "contentMarkdown": "For small altitudes h << R_e, acceleration due to gravity varies linearly as g(h) ~= g * [1 - 2*h/R_e].",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_5F4143EE_80768708"
          ]
        }
      ],
